GroovyMAME nogpu: RGB565 pixels and kernel pacing, for 100 Mbit beam-racing receivers

For Groovy_TaNGer (Tang Primer 20K: 100 Mbit, 32 lines of BRAM, no frame buffer). Both
options default to off, so a MiSTer client behaves as before.

-mister_rgb_mode rgb565 (default rgb888)
  Sends the 5th CMD_INIT byte the Groovy_MiSTer protocol already defines (rgbMode 2 = RGB565,
  2 bytes per pixel, little-endian; the MiSTer core implements it too). Raw RGB888 does not
  fit a 320x240@60 beam race on 100 Mbit (116 Mbit/s on the wire), RGB565 does (77 Mbit/s).
  rgb888 keeps the 4-byte CMD_INIT, so older servers are unaffected. Frame sizes (raw, lz4,
  lz4hc) use the bytes per pixel instead of a fixed 3.

-mister_pacing <F> (default 0 = off; suggested 1.0 to 1.02)
  Each CMD_SWITCHRES sets SO_MAX_PACING_RATE to F x (bytes per raster line incl. 42 bytes of
  headers per 1472-byte datagram) x hfreq. sendto() still returns at once, the emulation
  thread never waits, and the kernel's fq qdisc releases the datagrams at the raster's line
  rate instead of one wire-speed burst that overruns the receiver's ring.
  Linux can only LOWER a UDP socket's pacing rate (sk_pacing_rate = min(old, new)), so to
  raise it after a narrower mode the socket is reopened on the same local port.
  Linux only (elsewhere ignored with a verbose message). Needs:
    tc qdisc replace dev <if> root fq flow_limit 1000
  (fq_codel ignores the rate; fq's default flow_limit of 100 packets is less than a frame)
  and net.core.wmem_max >= 2 MB (the socket buffer holds a frame while it is paced out).

Replaces groovymame-mister-rgb565.patch, -pacing.patch and -pacing-raise-fix.patch.
Base: substring/GroovyMAME 4ce01b9 (libretro-driver). Apply with
  git apply groovymame-mister.patch
Checked: identical to the fork that ran on the board (2026-11-03, Sonic 2 on the CRT).

